<h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 mb-2">From a scanned PDF to a database and web application: AI agents in action</h1>
<p class="text-lg text-gray-500">Processing historical sources with the Chronos AI agent system.</p>
</header>
<div class="space-y-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
{% raw %}


<p><strong class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Case study:</strong> Paul Simson, <em>Danziger Inventar 1531–1591. Mit einem Akten-Anhang</em>, Munich and Leipzig, 1913. The digitised copy used in this project is available on the <a href="https://archive.org/details/danzigerinventar00simsuoft/page/n7/mode/2up" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Internet Archive</a>.</p>
<h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4" id="1-project-aims-and-stages">1. Project aims and stages</h2>
<p>A scanned book makes a source readable, but it does not yet offer a convenient way to bring together all the entries concerning a person, place or period. The aim of this project is to turn the published inventory into a dataset quickly, retaining its connection to the printed volume while enabling questions that go beyond simply browsing a PDF, through advanced searching and filtering.</p>
<p>The PDF contains scans of a book published in 1913. Its author, <a href="https://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Simson" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Paul Simson</a>, was a historian of Gdańsk. Although the PDF includes an OCR layer, this project was intended to demonstrate the capabilities of AI agent systems, so the text was read directly from the page images.</p>
<p>A historian working with an AI agent system might follow these stages:</p>
<pre class="block w-full p-4 bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-700 whitespace-pre-wrap break-words leading-relaxed"><code class="language-text">PDF containing scans
  → page images imported from the PDF into Chronos
  → entries transcribed into JSON
  → fragments merged and transcriptions checked
  → dates, place names and Polish translations
  → index of people and places, linked to entries
  → SQLite database
  → application for browsing, filtering and searching
</code></pre>
<p>JSON is a text-based data format: a single entry can be an object containing fields such as <code class="bg-gray-200 text-gray-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-sm break-words">date</code>, <code class="bg-gray-200 text-gray-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-sm break-words">text</code> and <code class="bg-gray-200 text-gray-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-sm break-words">entry_number</code>, while the inventory as a whole can be an array of these objects. SQLite is a lightweight SQL database that stores data in a file and allows entries to be linked to people, places and pages. A web application will make these data available to readers through a browser, with search and filtering facilities.</p>
<h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4" id="2-the-role-of-chronos">2. The role of Chronos</h2>
<p>Chronos is an AI agent system designed specifically to work with historians: extracting structured datasets from primary sources, adapting to heterogeneous documents and accumulating domain knowledge during the process. It was developed as part of <a href="https://ai-historian.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">The AI Historian</a>, a project funded by Schmidt Sciences’ AI2050 initiative and carried out by specialists from Schmidt Sciences, the University of Oxford and the University of Adelaide. Technically, Chronos is an extension for the Visual Studio Code development environment, working with the <code class="bg-gray-200 text-gray-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-sm break-words">pi</code> agent (<strong>a standalone application that can be used outside Visual Studio Code is also under development</strong>; after checking its current state and testing it, I will describe its installation in a separate blog post). An LLM interprets the instructions and source material, while the agent can organise tasks and use additional tools. In this project, the outputs also include scripts that process files: merging entries, tidying metadata and sending text for translation. The historian guides the processing of the source using natural language—in this case, Polish.</p>
<p>The developers describe Chronos as a tool for working with historians to produce structured data. Their project website includes an example involving a Frankfurt merchants’ directory from 1812 and a map of the city from 1811, resulting in an interactive map. See the <a href="https://ai-historian.com/research/chronos/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Chronos overview</a>.</p>
<figure class="my-8 space-y-3"><a aria-label="Open full-size image: The first page of the inventory on the left, with the conversation and task specification on the right" class="block" href="/assets/images/blog/chronos_01.png"><img alt="The first page of the inventory on the left, with the conversation and task specification on the right" class="mx-auto max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg border border-gray-200 shadow-sm" decoding="async" height="1304" loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/blog/chronos_01.png" width="1684"/></a><figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed italic">Figure 1. The workspace displays the scan alongside the instructions. The page image can be used to check the year, the beginnings of entries and their final reference numbers.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Alongside Visual Studio Code’s standard file explorer, the Chronos interface includes a viewer for source scans, a panel displaying the data extracted from the source, and a chat panel where the historian communicates with the agent.</p>
<h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4" id="3-preparing-the-project-and-importing-the-pdf">3. Preparing the project and importing the PDF</h2>
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-6 mb-3" id="installation-and-the-first-session">Installation and the first session</h3>
<p>To start using Chronos, install VS Code and Node.js according to the requirements in the current <a href="https://github.com/ai-historian/chronos#prerequisites" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Chronos README</a>. In VS Code’s Extensions view, search for <strong class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Chronos — The AI Historian</strong>, published by <strong class="font-semibold text-gray-900">AI-Historian</strong>. On first launch, the extension may offer to install the pi agent and the Chronos package.</p>
<p>Open the VS Code Command Palette with <code class="bg-gray-200 text-gray-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-sm break-words">Ctrl+Shift+P</code> and run:</p>
<ol class="list-decimal list-inside bg-gray-50 p-6 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 space-y-2">
<li><strong class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Chronos: Init Workspace</strong> — initialise a workspace in the project folder.</li>
<li><strong class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Chronos: Import Sources</strong> — select the PDF containing the book scans.</li>
<li><strong class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Chronos: Start Agent Session</strong> — open the Chronos workspace panel.</li>
<li>In the <strong class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Source</strong> field of the Chronos panel, select the imported source.</li>
</ol>
<p>Importing creates page images. In this project, they are stored in <code class="bg-gray-200 text-gray-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono text-sm break-words">sources/DANZIGER_INVENTAR_1531-1591/png/</code>. It is also worth retaining the original PDF and recording the provenance of the digitised copy. See the <a href="https://github.com/ai-historian/chronos#getting-started" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">getting-started instructions</a>.</p>
<p>A standalone application that can be used outside VS Code is also under development. This should make installation and use more accessible to historians: <a href="https://github.com/ai-historian/chronos-desktop" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">chronos-desktop</a>.</p>
